# Pre-Commit README Check

**Before creating any git commit** (when the user asks you to commit), review whether `README.md` needs updating for the changes being committed.

## When to Update README

Update `README.md` if the commit includes any of:

- New or removed **user-facing features** or routes
- Changes to **quick start**, env vars, Node/npm requirements, or hosting steps
- New **npm scripts**, game-data pipeline steps, or BP Dumper workflow changes
- New **Edge Functions**, Supabase migrations, or setup prerequisites
- Renamed, moved, or deprecated tools/pages members would look for in the README

Also check **`docs/SUPABASE_SETUP.md`** when the commit adds or changes migrations (`supabase/migrations/*.sql`) or Edge Function deploy steps.

## When README Update Is Not Needed

Skip README edits for:

- Bug fixes that do not change user-visible behavior or setup
- Internal refactors, styling-only changes, or data timestamp churn
- Officer/super-admin-only tooling with no member-facing impact

## Pre-Commit Checklist

Before `git commit`:

- [ ] Read `README.md` (and `docs/SUPABASE_SETUP.md` if migrations/setup changed)
- [ ] Compare against the staged diff — does the README still describe the product accurately?
- [ ] If updates are needed, include them **in the same commit** (or tell the user why you are splitting docs into a follow-up commit)
- [ ] Do not commit stale README claims (wrong migration numbers, missing features, outdated commands)

## README Focus

Write for **developers and site maintainers**, not in-app member help. Keep feature lists scannable; link to `docs/` for deep setup detail.
